//? Title: GJK Distance: Convex Hulls, Simplex Steps and Numerical Limits
//? Subtitle: Actual support queries, hand calculations and scale checks in native C++
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given two finite point clouds in the plane, how far apart are their convex hulls?
Checking all pairs of boundary features can be expensive; an iterative method can
instead ask each hull for its furthest vertex in a chosen direction. This report
shows that search on hand-checkable squares and a triangle, repeats the original
square–pentagon example, and measures actual support scans. Scale experiments also
show why this implementation's absolute tolerances make its returned distance an
approximation: zero need not certify contact, and a small overlapping pair can
return a positive value. Native C++ APIs produce every table, diagram and plot.
)

\toc

> From two hulls to one nearest point

For nonempty clouds A and B, write C=conv(A)−conv(B). A point in C is a difference
of two convex combinations. Minimizing its length gives the distance between the
hulls. A support query maximizes a linear functional; over a finite cloud the
maximum is attained at a stored vertex, so the complete hull need not be built.

$$
d=\min_{c\in C}\|c\|,\qquad
s_C(v)=s_A(v)-s_B(-v),\qquad
s_A(v)\in\underset{a\in A}{\operatorname{argmax}}\;a\cdot v.
$$
\alttext(The hull-to-hull distance is the minimum norm in their Minkowski difference. Its support point is a support point of A in direction v minus a support point of B in the opposite direction.)

The native API takes stored two-dimensional vertices, including unordered clouds,
duplicates, points and segments. A concave input is interpreted through its convex
hull. It does not accept arbitrary support callbacks, curved shapes or 3D bodies.
For empty input it retains the historical return value zero; distance between
nonempty geometric sets is the setting of the equation above.

Consider squares of half-width one, centered at (0,0) and (5,0). Their horizontal
intervals are [−1,1] and [4,6], so their gap is 4−1=3. Their Minkowski difference
is [−7,−3]×[−2,2], whose nearest point to the origin is (−3,0). The native trace
below shows the initial support, next support and actual retained features.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gjk_squares.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/gjk.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

 const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> a{{-1,-1},{1,-1},{1,1},{-1,1}};
 const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> b{{4,-1},{6,-1},{6,1},{4,1}};
 const auto t=dm::geometry::gjk_distance_trace(a,b);
 std::print("| event kind | iteration | point x | point y | active feature | value |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(const auto& e:t.events)
  std::print("| {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {} | {:.6f} |\n",int(e.kind),e.iteration,e.point.x,e.point.y,e.simplex_size,e.value);
 std::print("\nDistance {:.6f}; stop {}; support queries {}; vertex scans {}; records {}.\n",t.run.distance,dm::geometry::gjk_stop_name(t.run.stop),t.run.work.support_calls/2,t.run.work.support_dots,t.events.size());
 dm::graphics::Diagram d(68,1000);
 dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue;blue.color={37,99,235};
 dm::graphics::DiagramStroke orange;orange.color={217,119,6};
 d.polygon({{-1,3},{1,3},{1,5},{-1,5}},{219,234,254},blue)
  .polygon({{4,3},{6,3},{6,5},{4,5}},{254,243,199},orange)
  .text({0,5.6},"A: centre (0, 0)",22).text({5,5.6},"B: centre (5, 0)",22)
  .path({{1,4},{4,4}}).label({2.5,4},std::format("gap = {:.0f}",t.run.distance),22)
  .polygon({{0,-4},{4,-4},{4,0},{0,0}},{220,252,231})
  .text({2,.65},"A − B: [−7, −3] × [−2, 2]",22)
  .ellipse({7,-2},.09,.09,{15,23,42}).text({7,-2.6},"origin",20)
  .ellipse({7+t.run.closest.x,-2+t.run.closest.y},.09,.09,{5,150,105})
  .path({{7,-2},{7+t.run.closest.x,-2+t.run.closest.y}})
  .label({5.5,-2},std::format("distance = {:.0f}",t.run.distance),20)
  .text({2,-4.65},"nearest difference = (−3, 0)",20)
  .save_svg("build/gjk_squares.svg");
 if(t.run.distance!=3||t.run.closest.x!=-3)throw std::runtime_error("square gap");

}
