\image(figures/gjk_squares.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The upper view shows the two squares. The lower view translates the drawing origin to the right while keeping equal coordinate scales: the nearest Minkowski point is three units to its left. Event kinds are 0 initial, 1 direction, 2 support, 3 segment and 7 progress exit.)
\alttext(Two disjoint equal squares have a gap of three. Below them a rectangle representing the Minkowski difference lies three units left of its origin. The table retains every actual state record and the returned point (minus three, zero).)

The initial direction is A[0]−B[0]=(−5,0). First-index support ties give (−7,0).
Searching towards the origin then selects (−3,0), which replaces the two-point
simplex by its nearer endpoint. The next improvement is zero and the routine
returns three. The reported query count includes initialization; a query scans
both complete vertex lists.

> Project a segment, then retain a triangle

GJK keeps a simplex with at most three Minkowski points. Given the closest point
c of its current feature, it asks for a=s_C(−c). In exact arithmetic, the quantity
below is nonnegative for c in C. If it is zero, every z in C satisfies
c·(z−c)≥0. Expanding ||z||² then proves that c minimizes the norm. If it is
positive, the segment from c towards a initially decreases the squared norm.

$$
g(c)=\|c\|^2-c\cdot s_C(-c),\qquad
\frac{d}{dt}\|c+t(a-c)\|^2\bigg|_{t=0}=-2g(c).
$$
\alttext(The improvement is squared closest norm minus its dot product with the new support point. The derivative along the segment towards that support point is minus twice the improvement.)

Take A={(−1,−1),(1,−1),(0,1)} and B={(0,0)}. The first two selected points are
p=(−1,−1), q=(0,1). Minimizing their segment norm gives

$$
t=\operatorname{clamp}\!\left(\frac{-p\cdot(q-p)}{\|q-p\|^2},0,1\right)
=\frac35,\qquad c=p+t(q-p)=(-0.4,0.2).
$$
\alttext(The projection coefficient on the first selected segment is three fifths, producing closest point minus zero point four, zero point two.)

The next direction (0.4,−0.2) selects (1,−1). Those three selected points contain
the origin, so the triangle routine takes its inside branch. The table uses
records emitted by this executing kernel; it does not rerun the support or
simplex calculations to reconstruct a history.

The retained closest-feature region tests follow Christer Ericson’s
*Real-Time Collision Detection* (2005), Sections 5.1.2 and 5.1.5, as recorded in the
[author’s table of contents](https://realtimecollisiondetection.net/books/rtcd/toc/index.html).
They specialize the distance subproblem rather than implementing the paper’s
Johnson subalgorithm and backup procedure.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gjk_triangle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/gjk.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

 const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> a{{-1,-1},{1,-1},{0,1}},b{{0,0}};
 const auto t=dm::geometry::gjk_distance_trace(a,b);
 std::print("| event kind | selected A index | point x | point y | feature size |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(const auto& e:t.events)
  std::print("| {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {} |\n",int(e.kind),e.support_a,e.point.x,e.point.y,e.simplex_size);
 std::print("\nDistance {:.6f}; stop {}; segment reductions {}; triangle reductions {}; records {}.\n",t.run.distance,dm::geometry::gjk_stop_name(t.run.stop),t.run.work.segment_reductions,t.run.work.triangle_reductions,t.events.size());
 const auto it=std::find_if(t.events.begin(),t.events.end(),[](const auto& e){return e.kind==dm::geometry::GjkEventKind::segment;});
 if(it==t.events.end())throw std::runtime_error("missing actual segment");
 dm::graphics::Diagram d(100,1000);
 dm::graphics::DiagramStroke active;active.color={5,150,105};active.width=4;
 d.polygon({{-1,-1},{1,-1},{0,1}},{219,234,254})
  .path({{it->simplex[0].x,it->simplex[0].y},{it->simplex[1].x,it->simplex[1].y}},active)
  .ellipse({0,0},.045,.045,{15,23,42}).text({0,-.35},"origin",20)
  .ellipse({it->point.x,it->point.y},.045,.045,{5,150,105})
  .text({-1.25,.4},std::format("c = ({:.1f}, {:.1f})",it->point.x,it->point.y),20);
 dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=3.4;box.height=1.3;box.font_size=22;
 d.node("segment",{4,1},"Project the segment\nt = 3/5",box)
  .node("triangle",{4,-1.2},std::format("Next support: (1, −1)\n{}",dm::geometry::gjk_stop_name(t.run.stop)),box)
  .edge("segment","triangle","add support")
  .save_svg("build/gjk_triangle.svg");
 if(t.run.distance!=0||t.run.stop!=dm::geometry::GjkStopReason::triangle_contains_origin)throw std::runtime_error("triangle example");

}
