\image(figures/gjk_triangle.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The green segment joins the first two actual support points. Its green projection is (−0.4,0.2). Adding the next support point completes the surrounding triangle. Event kind 5 marks the actual floating triangle-inside branch.)
\alttext(A triangle surrounds the origin. Its left edge has a green closest point at minus zero point four, zero point two. Two connected boxes describe the segment projection and next support point. Every actual state is printed.)

[Gilbert, Johnson and Keerthi (1988)](https://doi.org/10.1109/56.2083) develop the
support composition and descent criterion in Sections III–IV; the
[original paper, page 196](https://graphics.stanford.edu/courses/cs164-09-spring/Handouts/paper_GJKoriginal.pdf)
states the exact-arithmetic optimality result. Their numerical procedure uses a
scale-dependent tolerance and a backup subalgorithm (Section VI). This library's
planar variant instead retains its original region tests, absolute cutoffs and
100-visit default cap. It supplies no exact floating containment certificate,
closest pair on the original shapes, penetration depth or time of impact.

> Repeat the original moving pentagon

The report's former local regular-polygon helper is now
`dm::geometry::regular_polygon()`. The square has circumradius one, rotation
0.3 radians and centre (0,0); the pentagon has circumradius one, rotation 0.1 and
centre (dx,0.4). The horizontal offset dx is not the Euclidean centre separation,
which is sqrt(dx²+0.4²). The four original distance calls and the complete
23-position sweep are retained below.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gjk_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/gjk.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

 const auto a=dm::geometry::regular_polygon({0,0},1,4,.3);
 std::print("| original horizontal offset | returned distance | stopping branch |\n| ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
 for(double dx:{1.5,2.,3.,5.}){
  const auto b=dm::geometry::regular_polygon({dx,.4},1,5,.1);
  const auto r=dm::geometry::gjk_distance_run(a,b);
  std::print("| {:.1f} | {:.4f} | {} |\n",dx,r.distance,dm::geometry::gjk_stop_name(r.stop));
 }
 std::vector<double> offset,gap;
 std::print("\n| sweep horizontal offset | returned distance |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(double dx=.5;dx<=6;dx+=.25){
  const auto b=dm::geometry::regular_polygon({dx,.4},1,5,.1);
  offset.push_back(dx);gap.push_back(dm::geometry::gjk_distance(a,b));
  std::print("| {:.2f} | {:.9f} |\n",dx,gap.back());
 }
 auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
 p.line(offset,gap,"gap",{5,150,105},2).points(offset,gap,"gap",{5,150,105},4)
  .legend({"Returned distance"},{{5,150,105}}).title("Original square and sliding pentagon").xlab("Horizontal offset dx").ylab("Returned distance")
  .x_limits(.5,6).y_limits(0,4.7).save_svg("build/gjk_original.svg");

}
```
