// result_end
\caption(At small scales the separated-square approximation can be zero or three times the true gap. Crossing segments can return a positive gap. The plotted normalized values expose errors that a broad absolute tolerance would hide.)
\alttext(Two panels show normalized gaps against log ten scale for all thirteen scales. The exact square gap divided by scale is two; the exact crossing-segment gap is zero. Returned values depart from both at small scales, including zero square gaps and positive crossing gaps.)

These are finite arithmetic results, not malformed inputs. The checked core keeps
them reproducible and exposes the actual stopping reason. It rejects nonfinite
coordinates and evaluated nonfinite intermediates, but finite underflow,
cancellation and approximate triangle predicates remain. A caller needing a
certified contact decision or a rigorous distance tolerance needs a method with
that explicit contract.

> Count actual support scans

A support query with n and m stored vertices performs n+m support dot products
and n+m−2 strict comparisons. Duplicate points still cost scans. Each iteration
uses at most one query; initialization uses another. With K loop visits,
validation plus search costs O((K+1)(n+m)); the working simplex and temporary
feature have at most three points, so ordinary auxiliary space is O(1).
History adds storage proportional to its actual complete record count.

This does not prove that unbounded GJK convergence takes a fixed number of
iterations. The scalar API's 100-visit cap guarantees termination by returning its
last approximation if needed. The following counts are actual selected kernel
operations, not timings, FLOPs or the complete cost of validation and allocation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gjk_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/gjk.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

 std::vector<double> sides,scans,queries;
 std::print("| sides in each hull | distance | loop visits | Minkowski queries | support dot products | strict comparisons |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(std::size_t n:{3u,4u,8u,16u,32u,64u,128u,256u,512u,1024u}){
  const auto a=dm::geometry::regular_polygon({0,0},1,n,.3),b=dm::geometry::regular_polygon({5,0},1,n,.1);
  const auto r=dm::geometry::gjk_distance_run(a,b);const auto q=r.work.support_calls/2;
  std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,r.distance,r.work.iterations,q,r.work.support_dots,r.work.support_comparisons);
  if(r.work.support_dots!=2*n*q||r.work.support_comparisons!=r.work.support_dots-r.work.support_calls)throw std::runtime_error("support accounting");
  sides.push_back(std::log2(double(n)));scans.push_back(std::log2(double(r.work.support_dots)));queries.push_back(double(q));
 }
 auto a=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),b=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
 a.line(sides,scans,"scans",{5,150,105},2).points(sides,scans,"scans",{5,150,105},4)
  .legend({"Support dot products"},{{5,150,105}}).title("All vertex scans, including initialization").xlab("log2(vertices per cloud)").ylab("log2(support dot products)").x_limits(1,10).y_limits(0,17);
 b.line(sides,queries,"queries",{37,99,235},2).points(sides,queries,"queries",{37,99,235},4)
  .legend({"Minkowski queries"},{{37,99,235}}).title("Actual query count depends on geometry").xlab("log2(vertices per cloud)").ylab("Minkowski queries").x_limits(1,10).y_limits(0,12);
 dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,510).title("A complete support scan is linear in the stored vertex count").fit_panels().save_svg("build/gjk_work.svg");

}
```
